title: Reading the log
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: guide
summary: mf log lists the revisions, newest first, --ops expands them; mf log show details one revision.

```
mf -n music log                                # the last 20 revisions
mf -n music log list --ops --limit 5           # … with their operations
mf -n music log list --metarecord 3f2a…        # only what touched one metarecord
mf -n music log list --since 2026-09-01 --until @1759000000000
mf -n music log show 12                        # one revision, before and after each change
mf -n music log show HEAD
mf -n music log head                           # the newest operation's id
```

`mf log` (`mf log list`) shows the log — a sequence, in the order it was written ([[The operation log]]) — one line per revision, newest first, the newest marked HEAD:

```
> rev 12  2024-01-15 14:32  "before-cleanup"   ← HEAD
  rev 11  2024-01-15 14:30  (5 ops: create_metarecord ×3, file_moved ×2)
  rev 10  2024-01-14 09:12
```

* `--ops` expands each revision into its operations (`op 23  set_field(rating)  on <uuid>`); a revision a revert wrote is tagged `(revert of rev N)`, and each of its operations names the one it undid.
* `--limit N` (default 20 revisions, or operations with `--ops`), `--all`, `--since`/`--until` (ISO-8601 or `@<unix ms>`), `--metarecord <uuid>` combine freely.
* A reconcile is one revision of maybe tens of thousands of operations; the listing reads at most 500, so such a revision may be shown cut: `[95 of 50000 operations shown]`.

`mf log show <id>` (or `HEAD`) prints each operation of one revision with its values
before and after; `--raw` the JSON. A `tree_ref` value shows its name, not a resolved
path. `mf log head` prints the newest operation's id — what `mf log undo --after` takes.

In the GUI: the [[log panel]], and the [[log-detail panel]] beside it for what a revision changed.
